# A variable made real (here by the decimal 0.5 added to it) keeps the real subtraction: only
# integer subtraction is truncated at 0. From r = 0.5, `r -= 1` gives -0.5 and `r += 1` gives
# 0.5, so the expectation is 0.5 and the upper bound 0.5 holds.
# Expected: valid. Truncating the real subtraction too would end at 1 and lose this answer.
r := 0;
r += 0.5;
r -= 1;
r += 1;
return r
?Ex[r] <= 0.5
